  "content": {
    "id": "v2-8a2d627b5-practice-slot-2-upper-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Determine where the function is discontinuous.\n$$f(x)=\\frac{x^2-4}{x^2-x-6}$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$x=-2,3$",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}x^2-x-6&=0\\\\(x-3)(x+2)&=0\\\\x&=3,-2\\end{aligned}$$\nThe original denominator excludes both values. Since\n$$f(x)=\\frac{(x-2)(x+2)}{(x-3)(x+2)},$$\ncancelling $x+2$ does not restore $x=-2$ to the domain: there is a hole at $(-2,\\tfrac45)$ and a vertical asymptote at $x=3$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": null
  },
